Found while doing #92. The worker fixed the instance it hit; this is the class of bug behind it.

t/lib/MockStore.pm ends MockGit with:

    sub AUTOLOAD {
      our $AUTOLOAD;
      return if $AUTOLOAD =~ /::DESTROY$/;
      return 1;
    }

So every method nobody thought to stub answers a true scalar. Not "unimplemented", not an error -- a plausible-looking success value.

What that cost in #92: the new context activity section calls list_refs and read_ref. AUTOLOAD handed it the scalar 1 as if it were a list of one ref, and then again as that refs content, which the section then tried to decode as a log entry. The section had to be given explicit stubs (sub list_refs { () }, sub read_ref { undef }) before its tests meant anything.

The same trap is armed for the next Git method a command class starts calling. #104 has just added one (is_tracked_under); any future test that drives a command through MockStore will get 1 from it -- "yes, the project owns this path" -- and pass while asserting the opposite of the truth. Note that is a plausible answer, not an obviously broken one, which is what makes it dangerous.

Fix direction, same shape as the KNOWN_LEAK allow-list t/121 grew for #38: make the double explicit about what it answers. Either drop AUTOLOAD entirely and stub what is actually used, or keep it as a croak ("MockGit has no stub for X -- add one") so a missing stub fails loudly at the call instead of silently succeeding. Whichever way, check the existing stubs while there: pull/push/fetch returning bare 1 is the same shape of guess, it just happens to be the right answer today.

Worth checking whether any current test passes only because of an AUTOLOAD answer. That is the part that decides whether this is cleanup or a real hole.
